<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">E. M. Forster</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1879–1970</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Edward Morgan Forster</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">E. M. Forster was an English novelist and essayist, author of A Room with a View (1908), Howards End (1910) and A Passage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> (1924). In 1921 he served as private secretary to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tukoji-rao-iii-of-dewas-senior">Tukoji Rao III of Dewas Senior</a>, an experience he described in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hill-of-devi">The Hill of Devi</a> (1953). A Passage to India, drawing on his Indian years, became a classic of writing about British rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/e-m-forster.jpg" alt="E. M. Forster" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dora Carrington (1893–1932) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:E._M._Forster_von_Dora_Carrington,_1924-25.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
